Fix an elliptic curve $E/\mathbb Q$. For every fixed real $t$, $$\lim_{Y\to\infty}\frac1{\#\mathcal D(Y)}
 \sum_{d\in\mathcal D(Y)}\exp\!\bigl(t\,r(E^{(d)})\bigr)
 =\frac{1+e^t}{2}.$$ For every fixed positive integer $m$, $$\lim_{Y\to\infty}\frac1{\#\mathcal D(Y)}
 \sum_{d\in\mathcal D(Y)}r(E^{(d)})^m=\frac12.$$

There are fixed positive integers $A,B$ such that, for every dyadic $M\geq1$, every $F\in\mathcal F$, every $t\in\mathbb R$, and $1/2\leq\sigma\leq2$, $$\label{analytic:L-moment}
 \frac1M\sum_{\substack{d\ \mathrm{admissible}\\M\leq|d|<2M}}
 |L_{F^{(d)}}(\sigma+it)|^2
 \ll_{\mathcal F}\log^B(3M)(1+|t|)^A.$$ For arbitrary signed squarefree twisting parameters and $H\geq1$, $$\label{analytic:prefix-moment}
 \sum_{\substack{0<|h|\leq H\\h\ \mathrm{squarefree}}}
 |L_{F^{(h)}}(\sigma+it)|^2
 \ll_{\mathcal F}H\log^B(3H)(1+|t|)^A.$$

With the same fixed integers $A,B$, every fixed smooth $G$ of compact support in $(0,\infty)$ satisfies $$\label{analytic:polynomial-bound}
 \frac1M\sum_{\substack{d\ \mathrm{admissible}\\M\leq|d|<2M}}
 |P_d(N,t;G)|^2
 \ll_{\mathcal F,G}
 \log^B(3M)(1+|t|)^A(1+N/M)^A
 \qquad(N>0).$$ The bound is uniform for fixed uniformly smooth families of weights with common compact support.

Let $r$ be positive and odd, and write $h=h_1h_2^2\ne0$, where $h_1$ is signed squarefree and $h_2\geq1$. For $\nu\in\{1,-1\}$, the series $$\mathcal G_{h,r}^{\nu}(s_1,s_2)=
 \sum_{\substack{n_1,n_2\geq1\\n_1,n_2\ \mathrm{odd}}}
 \frac{\lambda_F(n_1)\lambda_F(n_2)B_{\nu h}(rn_1n_2)}
      {n_1^{s_1}n_2^{s_2}}$$ initially converges in a right half-plane and continues to $\Re s_1,\Re s_2>1/2$ as $$\label{analytic:euler-factorization}
 \mathcal G_{h,r}^{\nu}(s_1,s_2)=
 L_{F^{(\nu h_1)}}(s_1)L_{F^{(\nu h_1)}}(s_2)
 \mathcal Q_{h,r}^{\nu}(s_1,s_2).$$ For $0<b\leq1/4$ and $\Re s_i\geq1/2+b$, $$\label{analytic:euler-bound}
 |\mathcal Q_{h,r}^{\nu}(s_1,s_2)|
 \ll_{\mathcal F} b^{-C_{\rm ar}}r^{C_{\rm ar}}
                       \tau(h_2)^{C_{\rm ar}}.$$ The function $\mathcal Q$ is holomorphic in these open half-planes.

Let $U>0$, let $r$ be odd and positive, and let $N_i$ be bounded below by a fixed positive constant. Fix $0<b\leq1/4$. In the expression $$\label{analytic:poisson-average}
 \frac1U\sum_{m\in\mathbb Z}
 \sum_{\substack{n_1,n_2\geq1\\n_1,n_2\ \mathrm{odd}}}
 \frac{\lambda_F(n_1)\lambda_F(n_2)}
 {(n_1n_2)^{1/2}n_1^{it_1}n_2^{it_2}}
 \left(\frac m{rn_1n_2}\right)
 W(n_1/N_1,n_2/N_2,m/U),$$ Poisson summation gives the zero frequency, namely the complete-residue average integrated in $x$, together with the nonzero frequencies. For every fixed $J_1>0$, a sufficiently large fixed seminorm order $D$ bounds the absolute contribution of frequency $h=h_1h_2^2\ne0$ by $$\begin{aligned}
 &C_{J_1,\mathcal F}\|W\|_{D,*}
 (N_1N_2)^{-1/2+b}b^{-C_{\rm ar}}r^{C_{\rm ar}}
 \tau(h_2)^{C_{\rm ar}}
 \left(1+\frac{|h|U}{rN_1N_2}\right)^{-J_1}
 \nonumber\\[-2pt]
 &\hspace{8mm}\cdot
 \sum_{\nu=\pm1}\int_{\mathbb R^2}
 \frac{\prod_{i=1}^2
 |L_{F^{(\nu h_1)}}(1/2+b+i(t_i+v_i))|}
 {(1+|v_1|+|v_2|)^{J_1}}\,dv_1\,dv_2.
 \label{analytic:nonzero-mode}
\end{aligned}$$ If the Fourier transform of $W$ in $x$ is supported in a fixed interval $[-C_W,C_W]$, all modes with $|h|>C_Wa_1^2rN_1N_2/U$ vanish exactly.

For fixed $B\geq0$, $J_1>1$, and $Q>0$, $$\label{analytic:dyadic-frequency-bound}
 \sum_{T=1,2,4,\ldots}T\log^B(3T)(1+T/Q)^{-J_1}
 \ll_{B,J_1}
 \begin{cases}
 Q^{J_1},&0<Q<1,\\
 Q\log^B(3Q),&Q\geq1.
 \end{cases}$$ In particular the first bound is at most a constant times $Q$.

Let $d$ be admissible, $X\le |d|<2X$, and $x=d/X$. Set $$g_d(z)=L_{F^{(d)}}(1/2+z)(C_F|d|)^z\Gamma(1+z),
 \qquad \epsilon_d=\operatorname{sign}(F^{(d)}).$$ Then $g_d$ is entire, $g_d(-z)=\epsilon_d g_d(z)$, and $$\begin{split}
 \mathcal S_d
 &:=
 \sum_{n\ge1}\frac{\lambda(n)\chi_d(n)}{\sqrt n}
                 W_{k,x}(\ell^{-1}\log n)\\
 &=\frac{k!}{\ell^k}\frac{1}{2\pi i}
       \int_{(2)}g_d(z)\frac{dz}{z^{k+1}}.
 \end{split}
 \label{weights:completed-integral}$$ More precisely, $$\bigl(1+\epsilon_d(-1)^k\bigr)\mathcal S_d
       =\ell^{-k}g_d^{(k)}(0).
 \label{weights:exact-derivative}$$ Consequently, if $a(F^{(d)})>k$ and $\epsilon_d=(-1)^k$, then $$\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{\lambda(n)\chi_d(n)}{\sqrt n}
                  W_{k,d/X}(\ell^{-1}\log n)=0.
 \label{weights:zero}$$

After increasing the fixed $k_0$, one sufficiently large lower bound on $X$ makes $$\frac1X\sum_{\substack{d\ {\rm admissible}\\X\le|d|<2X}}
       |S_{*,\chi_d}(d/X)|^2
       \ll 2^{-3Jk/2}
 \label{weights:terminal-small-square}$$ simultaneously for all $k$ in <a href="#weights:range" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="weights:range">[weights:range]</a>. Moreover, for any fixed $c,L>0$, the comparison errors obey $$e^{-c\Delta_j\ell}
       \le k^{-L}2^{-Ljk}\qquad(0\le j\le J)
 \label{weights:comparison-absorption}$$ for one sufficiently large lower bound on $X$.

Let $\mathcal I$ be any set of primes at most $Y_0$, and put $$B_{\mathcal I}(V)=\sum_{p\in\mathcal I}
                 \lambda(p)V(p)p^{-1/2-s}.$$ If $k_0$ is a sufficiently large absolute constant, then $$\frac1X\sum_{X\le|m|<2X}|B_{\mathcal I}(z_m)|^{64}
 \ll\left(1+\sum_{p\in\mathcal I}\frac1p\right)^{32}
 \label{model:64th-moment}$$ uniformly in $\mathcal I,k,X$.

Except for $O(Xk^{-2})$ integers in $X\le|m|<2X$, the following inequalities hold simultaneously: $$\begin{aligned}
 \frac12\le H_j(z_m)P_j^{\mathrm{eu}}(z_m)&\le2
                         &&(0\le j\le J),
 \label{model:good-mollifier}\\
 \frac{P_0^{\mathrm{eu}}(z_m)}{P_j^{\mathrm{eu}}(z_m)}
   &\ge2^{-jk/8} &&(1\le j\le J),
 \label{model:good-ratio}\\
 P_0^{\mathrm{eu}}(z_m)&\ge2^{-Jk/8}.
 \label{model:good-full-product}
\end{aligned}$$ The implied constant and the lower threshold $k_0$ do not depend on $k,j,X$.
